  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "title": "hook (does a proton have parts?)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "We've treated protons and neutrons as solid little balls. But are they fundamental, or made of something smaller? How could you even tell, for something this small? Make your prediction. Fire particles at it, just as alpha particles were fired at gold to find the nucleus. So what evidence shows that protons and neutrons have smaller constituents?"
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 3,
      "holdKind": "countdown",
      "planningResponseInterval": {
        "countdownSeconds": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "criterion",
      "title": "what would count as evidence?",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "First, what would fundamental mean? A fundamental particle has no parts, and no size at all, as far as we can tell. So evidence against it would show either parts inside, or a size and a shape. Keep that test in mind, because every piece of evidence today is checked against it."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "probe",
      "title": "a probe small enough",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "To see inside something, your probe needs a wavelength smaller than it. Remember, wavelength is h over p, so more momentum means a shorter wavelength. In the late nineteen sixties, a three kilometre linear accelerator in California fired electrons at protons with twenty billion electron volts of energy. Their wavelength was about thirty times smaller than a proton. Electrons were a good choice, because they don't feel the strong force, and they behave like points, so any structure they reveal belongs to the target."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "observation",
      "title": "what they saw",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's what they saw. Most electrons were deflected only a little. But far more of them bounced off at large angles than a smooth proton could ever produce. It was the same surprise as alpha particles bouncing back from gold, one level deeper."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "inference",
      "title": "what it means",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now what it means. A smooth, spread-out proton can only nudge an electron gently. Hard, large-angle bounces mean the electron hit something small and concentrated inside the proton. The pattern fitted a proton containing a few tiny, point-like parts. So the proton has parts, and the first box on our test is ticked."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "hadron-patterns",
      "title": "too many particles",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The second clue came from numbers. By the nineteen sixties, experiments had found hundreds of particles that feel the strong force, called hadrons. Hundreds is far too many to all be fundamental. And they fell into neat families, with patterns in their charges and masses. Remember the periodic table. Its patterns came from atoms being built from a few parts. These patterns pointed the same way."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "neutron-change",
      "title": "a neutron that changes",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's a tempting third clue that doesn't work. A free neutron decays into a proton, an electron and an antineutrino. But decay alone can't prove parts, because some fundamental particles decay too, like the muon you'll meet soon. Still, the quark model describes neutron decay neatly. One of its quarks changes type."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "quark-charges",
      "title": "the quark model",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "In nineteen sixty-four, Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig proposed that hadrons are made of quarks. The up quark has a charge of plus two thirds, and the down quark has minus one third. A proton is up, up, down. Two thirds plus two thirds minus one third is plus one. A neutron is up, down, down, which gives zero. One pair of values fits both nucleons, so the model isn't just fitted to one result. Quarks have never been found alone. Pull them apart, and the energy makes new quarks instead."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "electron-contrast",
      "title": "the same test on an electron",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's an important contrast. Fire the same kind of probe at an electron, and it shows no structure at all, as far as anyone has measured. It still behaves like a point. So the evidence doesn't say everything has parts. It separates protons and neutrons, which do, from electrons, which don't."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "positron-track",
      "title": "a track that bent the wrong way",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the second half of the question. What evidence showed other particles exist? In nineteen thirty-two, cosmic rays passed through a cloud chamber, a box of vapour where a charged particle leaves a trail of droplets, inside a magnetic field. A lead plate across the middle slowed particles down, and a slower particle curves more tightly. So the tighter side showed which way it was travelling. Knowing the direction, the way it bent meant positive charge. But the trail looked like an electron's. It was the positron, the electron's antimatter partner."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "muon-pion",
      "title": "heavier cousins",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "More surprises followed. In nineteen thirty-six, cosmic-ray tracks revealed a particle like an electron, but about two hundred times heavier, the muon. In nineteen forty-seven, photographic plates carried up mountains caught the pion. Then accelerators began making new particles by the hundred."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "neutrino",
      "title": "the particle no one could see",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "And one particle was found by what was missing. In beta decay, the electrons come out with a spread of energies, not one fixed value. Energy seemed to vanish. In nineteen thirty, it was proposed that an unseen, neutral particle carried the rest. Here it's an antineutrino, one of the neutrino family. It was finally detected in nineteen fifty-six, right beside a nuclear reactor, where enormous numbers of them pour out."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "two-families",
      "title": "two families",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "So particles sort into two families. Hadrons feel the strong force, and they're made of quarks. Baryons, like protons and neutrons, have three quarks. Mesons, like the pion, have a quark and an antiquark, the antimatter partner of a quark. Leptons don't feel the strong force, and they have no known parts. The electron, the muon and the neutrinos are leptons. A pion is light, but it's still a hadron."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "memory-aid",
      "title": "a way to remember",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's a way to remember the split. Hadrons have parts. Leptons are points."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 2,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
